Sval: GTK commits the toplevel's new opaque region on Wayland

nsWindow::UpdateOpaqueRegionInternal sets the toplevel GdkWindow's opaque
region, which GDK sends with the toplevel surface's next commit. GTK
commits that surface only when it repaints it, and a window that draws
its own titlebar has nothing of GTK's to repaint. So a region that
shrinks, as when the chrome turns translucent, never reaches the
compositor, which keeps drawing the toplevel's clear pixels as opaque
black under the translucent parts. Invalidating the toplevel makes GTK
commit it.

diff --git a/widget/gtk/nsWindow.cpp b/widget/gtk/nsWindow.cpp
index 6230e46d0d..eedc4b8b83 100644
--- a/widget/gtk/nsWindow.cpp
+++ b/widget/gtk/nsWindow.cpp
@@ -5107,6 +5107,11 @@ void nsWindow::UpdateOpaqueRegionInternal() {
 #ifdef MOZ_WAYLAND
     if (GdkIsWaylandDisplay()) {
       mSurface->SetOpaqueRegion(mOpaqueRegion.ToUnknownRegion());
+      // GDK sends the toplevel's opaque region with the toplevel's next
+      // commit, and GTK commits it only when it repaints it. Until then the
+      // compositor draws the toplevel's clear pixels as opaque black under
+      // every part of the window that turned translucent.
+      gdk_window_invalidate_rect(window, nullptr, false);
     }
 #endif
   }
